- NUMA-aware page replacement decisions
- Interaction with swap and lazy reclaim mechanisms
- Concurrency-safe quota accounting
- Starvation prevention and thrashing avoidance

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal C-like skeleton of reclaim loop
for (each node in nodes) {
    while (deficit[node] > 0) {
        // Select overcommitted cgroup as victim
        struct cgroup *victim = pick_victim_cgroup(node);

        // Evict pages from victim (prefer inactive, anon > file)
        struct page *page = lru_scan(victim, node);
        if (page) {
            if (try_to_reclaim(page))
                deficit[node] -= PAGE_SIZE;
        }
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of timer data structures, concurrency, and practical OS scheduling under contention.

## Key Concepts
- Timer wheel: bucketed deadlines for O(1) insert
- Concurrency: per-bucket locking or lock-free heads
- Expiration path: wake task, reschedule, handle cancellations

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Timer {
  uint64_t expiry; // system ticks
  struct Task* t;
  struct Timer* next;
} Timer;

#define WHEEL_SIZE 256

typedef struct TimerWheel {
  Timer* buckets[WHEEL_SIZE];
  uint64_t current;
  spinlock_t lock;
} TimerWheel;

void insert_timer(TimerWheel* w, Timer* tm, uint64_t expiry) {
  uint64_t now = get_current_tick();
  uint64_t delta = (expiry > now) ? (expiry - now) : 0;
  uint64_t idx = (w->current + (delta % WHEEL_SIZE)) % WHEEL_SIZE;
  tm->expiry = expiry;
  tm->next = w->buckets[idx];
  w->buckets[idx] = tm;
}
```

